  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY NIGHT THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 220 PM MDT Sun Sep 27 2026\n\nTuesday night through the end of the work week could see a few\nareas of showers trying to lift up into the region, but this\nremains largely uncertain and low confidence as models remain\nsplit on the evolution of low pressure to our south and how far\nnorth moisture can make it. While some ensembles have begun to\npush the low and moisture associated with what will be the \nremnants of Hurricane Polo further northwards compared to the \nlast few runs, overall this system still looks to be too far \nsouth to bring any further appreciable moisture to our area \nbeyond Tuesday outside of maybe our farthest southeast zones, \nprimarily Cheyenne County in the Nebraska Panhandle. The NBM \ncontinues to keep us high dry as well, with the above scenario \nremaining in the grids as of this writing. Still through \nThursday POP chances aren't completely zero, but outside of the \nI-80 corridor and southward it's not looking favorable for any \nmeaningful moisture in the long term. Otherwise we'll see a very\nslight warming trend on Wednesday, with temperatures warming a \nfew degrees with widespread 60's and a few 70's expected, but a \nsecond cold airmass should drop us right back down into the \nwidespread 60's on Thursday. Friday and Saturday high pressure \nover the southwestern US will allow for a warming and drying \ntrend, returning us to several degrees above normal with highs \nin the 70's across the region. Sunday flow turns more \nnorthwesterly and a glancing upper level trough will help push a\nweak cold front into the region, bringing temperatures back \ndown to near normal, but a lack of notable moisture will keep \nour precipitation chances almost nil and provide little more \nthan a brief cool down to end the long term forecast.",
